#!bovnar 1.1
# cf.bvnr — single source of truth for the CF standard-name profile.
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# READ THIS FIRST: A STANDARD NAME IS NOT A UNIT
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# `cf:air_temperature` does not spell a unit. It names WHAT IS BEING MEASURED,
# the way qudt-qk.bvnr's quantity kinds do, and the reasoning there applies here
# unchanged: a value_unit_t is a product of prefixed base units, so a namespace
# that admits a quantity name has to answer with a unit or refuse the name.
#
# It answers with the unit CF ITSELF STATES. Every entry in the standard name
# table carries a `canonical_units` field -- a UDUNITS expression, because CF's
# unit syntax is UDUNITS -- and that field, not this file's reading of the name,
# is what each row translates. `air_temperature` says "K", so `cf:air_temperature`
# is the kelvin. `northward_wind` says "m s-1", so it is the metre per second.
#
# WHAT A PRODUCER GETS OUT OF IT, GIVEN THAT
#   A netCDF/CF variable carries `standard_name` and `units` as two attributes,
#   and the units attribute is optional exactly when the standard name fixes it.
#   A pipeline that has the name and not the units can therefore write the value
#   down without inventing a units string, and a reader gets the same
#   value_unit_t either way -- `cf:air_temperature` and `udunits:K` translate to
#   the same unit and compare equal.
#
#   THE BITE IS THE SAME ONE qudt-qk HAS. A producer whose numbers are not in
#   the canonical units will write a wrong value that parses cleanly. If your
#   temperatures are in degrees Celsius, `cf:air_temperature` is the wrong thing
#   to write and `udunits:degC` is the right one. CF says the same thing in its
#   own conventions: the units attribute must be convertible to the canonical
#   units, and a value that is not in them needs that attribute.
#
# THIS PROFILE IS READ-ONLY, AND THAT IS NOT A LIMITATION BUT THE POINT
#   bvn_unit_to_profile("cf", u) returns -1 for every unit. There is nothing it
#   could honestly return: 4450 standard names translate to about a hundred
#   distinct units, so "the kelvin" would have to be written back as one
#   arbitrarily chosen name out of the sixty-nine that state K -- and that name
#   would assert what the quantity IS, which the unit does not know. Writing
#   `cf:air_temperature` for a sea-surface temperature is not a formatting
#   choice, it is a false statement. The registry marks the profile unwritable
#   (gen_profiles.py), so no reverse table is generated at all rather than one
#   row being singled out to be wrong.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT
#   * The 599 ALIASES. CF keeps deprecated names in the table as `<alias>`
#     entries pointing at their replacements. They are not spellings this
#     profile blesses: a document written today should carry the current name,
#     and an alias fails as error_unit_illegal, which says so.
#   * Every name whose canonical_units is `1`, `1e-3`, `1e-6` or empty -- 621 of
#     them, the largest single group. Dimension one is spelled in bovnar by
#     omitting the unit slot, and a bare scale factor -- "1e-3", which CF gives
#     `sea_water_salinity` and its neighbours -- is a magnitude with no unit at
#     all. Translating either to some dimensionless native unit would put a
#     salinity, a percentage and a bit in one equivalence class. The empty
#     canonical_units in that group are the STRING-valued names (`region`,
#     `area_type`, `platform_name`), which label a variable rather than measure
#     one.
#   * `dBZ`, the radar reflectivity decibel: a logarithm against a reference
#     (1 mm⁶/m³) that bovnar cannot state, the same argument doc/11 makes for
#     the neper and pH.
#   * Anything canonically measured per YEAR. CF's year is UDUNITS', the
#     tropical 31556925.9747 s; bovnar's `yr` is the Julian 31557600 s, and
#     doc/11 section 13.3 already refuses the UDUNITS one for the same reason.
#
# HOW THIS WORKS
#   Hand-edited; gen_profiles.py regenerates the C tables. A FLAT profile: a
#   standard name is one whole token, matched entire. There are no operators to
#   parse and no prefix to apply -- `cf:kilo_air_temperature` is not a thing.
#
#   Only the ~116 DISTINCT canonical_units strings were mapped by hand; every
#   row then takes the target its own canonical_units names, so two standard
#   names that state the same units cannot drift apart. check_profile_factors.py
#   re-evaluates each canonical_units string with the UDUNITS evaluator it
#   already uses for the udunits profile and compares that against the native
#   target -- CF's file and Unidata's file, both primary, on the two sides of
#   every row.
#
# PROVENANCE
#   CF standard name table version 94 (2026-06-09), the
#   `current` table published at cfconventions.org.
#
# SOURCE, VERSION AND LICENSING
#   Vocabulary  CF standard name table v94, last modified 2026-06-09
#   Verified    against that table, retrieved 2026-07-30 from
#               https://cfconventions.org/Data/cf-standard-names/94/src/
#               cf-standard-name-table.xml -- the VERSIONED path, not
#               `current/`, so this row set stays reproducible after the
#               table moves on
#   Publisher   maintained on behalf of the CF community by the Centre for
#               Environmental Data Analysis (CEDA)
#   Licence     NONE STATED BY THE PUBLISHER. The CF conventions DOCUMENT is
#               CC0 1.0, but this table is maintained separately, in
#               cf-convention/vocabularies, which carries no licence file and
#               no in-band rights statement
#   Taken       standard names, and the canonical_units each name states --
#               that field is carried in a trailing comment on every row and IS
#               the row's target, so it cannot be paraphrased away. No CF
#               <description> text
#   Modified    yes -- a subset, each name translated to the unit its own
#               canonical_units field states
#
#   Nothing suggests the CF community means the table to be restricted -- it
#   exists to be embedded in data files worldwide -- but there is no grant to
#   point at, so THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md attributes it and records the request
#   for an explicit licence. A build that must not carry the table at all takes
#   -DBVNR_WITH_CF_PROFILE=OFF.

.schema_version = <uint:16> 1;

# 4450 standard names, alphabetical. The `.bovnar` target of each is
# the unit its own canonical_units states; no row carries `.reverse`
# because the profile is unwritable and no reverse table is built.
